WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:02.910
Pooja Tewari: This is not how
I thought I would end college.

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:04.560
Daniela Beck: My school where I'm from

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.760
has really been impacted by the virus.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.040
Emma O'Brien: We expected
something to happen soon,

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.331
but we didn't know it would be

00:00:10.331 --> 00:00:12.881
this drastic so quickly.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.050
Masha Pescheryakova: When my chancellor

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:21.383
first sent out an email

00:00:21.383 --> 00:00:24.580
telling us that UCSB's going remote,

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.450
I was shocked.

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:28.896
Hasumi Tani: Students who were off campus

00:00:28.896 --> 00:00:30.928
during spring break were essentially told

00:00:30.928 --> 00:00:33.461
to not come back to campus,

00:00:33.461 --> 00:00:35.580
and they were ordered to either stay

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.750
or go back to their permanent address.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.320
Daniela Dwek: Last week was my
last time in the facilities.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.240
Belkis Anane: School is closed.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.120
Students were instructed to evacuate

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:44.670
and join their families.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:48.460
But I cannot, because I'm
an international student,

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.630
and I cannot leave because of travel bans.

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:51.630
Hasumi: As an international student,

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.510
I do not have a place to stay here,

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:57.270
and, currently, I'm in a
pretty sticky situation.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.420
My visa was rejected this week,

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:01.795
so I literally

00:01:01.795 --> 00:01:04.236
do not have a place to go back right now.

00:01:04.236 --> 00:01:06.640
And my only choice is to stay

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.930
here in America, and hopefully Georgetown.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:11.640
Preet Kaur: I have chosen
to stay in Santa Cruz.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:15.980
This is mainly because if
I am exposed to the virus

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.410
and I'm asymptomatic right now,

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:20.370
I don't want to go back
to my parents' house

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:22.090
and accidentally expose my family.

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:23.070
Huma Manjra: When I came home yesterday,

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.800
I had a lot of paranoia around,

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.190
I really just sat through a flight,

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.820
I really just went through
two of the biggest airports,

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:30.636
and who knows what I touched,

00:01:30.636 --> 00:01:33.194
and I had this fear
that, OK, if I come home,

00:01:33.194 --> 00:01:34.500
am I gonna give anything to my parents,

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.840
am I gonna give anything
to my older, like,

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.250
relatives, like my grandparents?

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.780
Being here in LA County,
where the cases are soaring,

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:43.110
and doubling and tripling with each day,

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:45.970
is really heightening my fear.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:49.180
Genevieve Korn: I may not
be able to see my parents

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:52.870
for an extended period of
time. Due to their age,

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:56.010
they are in one of the at-risk categories,

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:57.900
and I'm very afraid that the next time

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.970
I look at my phone someone I
know and love will be gone.

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:04.136
Belkis: Few of us are left here,

00:02:04.136 --> 00:02:07.102
but everyone I know and care about

00:02:07.102 --> 00:02:09.490
is halfway across the globe.

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:11.520
So it is definitely very hard

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.090
to go through this on your own.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.648
Housing is not a problem because

00:02:14.648 --> 00:02:16.623
I already live off campus,

00:02:16.623 --> 00:02:18.400
but I believe it is a great challenge

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.820
to see all of my roommates and classmates

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:22.595
leave and join their families

00:02:22.595 --> 00:02:24.536
while I cannot do that.

00:02:24.536 --> 00:02:27.320
It is very, very hard to be productive

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:29.590
and work online when you are very worried

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:32.550
for your family, who's in
a very serious outbreak.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.115
AJ Yablonsky: The whole
university is now pass/fail,

00:02:34.115 --> 00:02:36.230
mandatory. You can't even
opt in to take letter grade,

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:37.960
because for people who are going home

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:40.310
to family situations that
they have to take care

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:42.560
of a loved one who's
sick with this illness

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.018
or, you know, provide
for their family because

00:02:45.018 --> 00:02:46.390
someone might have lost a job,

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:47.610
they can't worry about
their grades right now.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.520
Genevieve: No one knows what's happening.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.210
But in terms of the professors
and the universities,

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:53.810
they're doing the best they can.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:56.010
They don't know how to cope just as much

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.130
as we don't know how to cope.

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:01.460
Emma: It is, you know,
really difficult to cater

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:03.760
to all of the needs of students online,

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.570
and I think a lot of
classes will struggle.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:10.090
We really rely on the student to professor

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:11.510
contact and relationship,

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.643
and I just don't think that's

00:03:13.643 --> 00:03:15.359
really possible online.

00:03:15.359 --> 00:03:17.980
Amona Saleh: Having our
classes being moved to online

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:21.300
has definitely lessened the
quality of our education.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.390
Like, my science classes, all of our labs

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.830
have been just canceled.

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:27.955
Emily Herrmann: What really
stood out to me is just

00:03:27.955 --> 00:03:30.205
how radically unprepared

00:03:30.205 --> 00:03:32.410
the education system was for this switch.

00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:34.900
I know of some professors
in the humanities

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:36.375
who didn't even use email

00:03:36.375 --> 00:03:38.566
before all of this went down.

00:03:38.566 --> 00:03:40.030
Genevieve: There are a
lot of students out there

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:41.790
that are really feeling lost right now

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:43.760
and really need a friend.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.670
And they need someone to talk to

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:48.590
because everything they knew was

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:52.300
ripped away from them in a set of emails.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:54.920
Preet: A lot of extracurriculars on campus

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:57.850
have also been totally eliminated.

00:03:57.850 --> 00:03:59.710
I personally do research in a lab,

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:04.120
and I am not allowed to
go on campus currently

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:08.190
unless I have things that
I need to keep alive.

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:09.560
Emily: I'm an athlete on the track team.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:11.813
Our entire spring season got canceled,

00:04:11.813 --> 00:04:13.010
which has been really tough.

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:15.170
Genevieve: An organization
that I'm the president of,

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:17.510
all of our competitions were canceled,

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:20.880
which I understand why for
the safety of everyone,

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.130
I agree with that they were canceled.

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:24.970
I cried a lot when I found out

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:26.630
that all of the work we had put in

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:28.830
for months and months and months

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.620
wasn't gonna happen this year.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:33.370
Pooja: I am in my final
semester of college,

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:36.030
I was supposed to be abroad,
had that taken away from me.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:39.040
I'm supposed to be
graduating, walking in May,

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.650
and there's a chance my
commencement ceremony

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:41.510
could get canceled.

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:43.490
AJ: Today actually we just got an email

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:47.360
that our commencement
ceremonies have been canceled.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:48.876
It feels surreal. Like, it's...

00:04:48.876 --> 00:04:51.609
I'm not even, like, sad.

00:04:51.609 --> 00:04:53.027
And I know I should be
sad and disappointed,

00:04:53.027 --> 00:04:55.077
and I guess I am to an extent,

00:04:55.077 --> 00:04:56.235
but it doesn't feel real.

00:04:56.235 --> 00:04:57.993
Huma: Just having to pick up,

00:04:57.993 --> 00:04:59.493
you know, leave everything,

00:04:59.493 --> 00:05:02.600
has been very, it's been
a lot for me to process.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:04.480
Emily: Not getting to say
goodbye to friends in person

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.740
has led to a lot of really emotional

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:09.790
FaceTime calls and texting calls

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:12.710
just thanking them for being
a friend for four years.

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:15.392
And I should have said stuff

00:05:15.392 --> 00:05:18.250
before coronavirus happened,
and I'm learning that.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:20.755
Elizabeth Camele: I go
through periods where I feel

00:05:20.755 --> 00:05:23.263
very angry about the situation,

00:05:23.263 --> 00:05:24.788
and I also go through periods

00:05:24.788 --> 00:05:26.700
where I'm just in shock.

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:28.700
Like, I can't believe this has happened.

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:30.770
Emma: So, a high moment of this experience

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:33.020
is, you know, just the sense of

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:35.261
community and love and support.

00:05:35.261 --> 00:05:37.573
Daniela: Going on
Facebook, different pages

00:05:37.573 --> 00:05:39.880
have been really uplifting
with other students

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:42.420
and new graduates being
in the same position.

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:45.880
And it's making me feel a lot better.

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:48.870
Katrina Jackson: They're
just a nice dose of humor

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:52.350
and a place for all the students

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:55.490
that are more or less
my age to come together

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:57.770
in solidarity and just laugh about things

00:05:57.770 --> 00:06:01.005
and provide support if
things are more serious.

00:06:01.005 --> 00:06:03.005
Daniela: Like, we're all in this together,

00:06:03.005 --> 00:06:05.646
and the class of 2020 will be remembered,

00:06:05.646 --> 00:06:07.690
for sure, with all of this.

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:11.380
And we will be celebrated
one day or another,

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:15.100
even if it's months after this pandemic.

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:16.470
Genevieve: It's terrible.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:19.755
I may not get to have
a graduation ceremony.

00:06:19.755 --> 00:06:21.890
I may not get to see
some of my friends again,

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:23.690
but in the end it hopefully

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:26.905
will be because lives were saved.

